// Port Arthur, the night of 8-9 February 1904 (real): the war's first blow, before its declaration. The Russian Pacific
// Squadron lies at anchor in the outer roads of Port Arthur, lit, its nets not out, two destroyers on picket; Togo sends
// in his destroyers from the east. History: the three flotillas came in about 23:30 local, lost touch with each other on
// meeting the pickets and attacked one by one into the searchlights; 16 torpedoes, 3 hits (Retvizan, Tsesarevich,
// Pallada, each holed and beached, months out of the war), no Japanese boat lost.
// The forces are history's: seven battleships, six cruisers, two pickets; ten destroyers. The classes: poltava for
// Petropavlovsk, Poltava and Sevastopol, oslyabya for Peresvet and Pobeda (her sisters), borodino for Tsesarevich (her
// near-sister) and Retvizan, aurora for Pallada and Diana (her sisters) and Askold, novik for Novik and Boyarin, bayan for
// Bayan, buyny for the pickets, ikazuchi for every Japanese boat.
//   Japan, the knife: ten 30 kn boats, two tubes each, the dark, and a fleet that doesn't know it's at war.
//   Russia, the giant asleep: fifteen ships and a hundred guns, unprepared, crews half trained, every ship stopped.
// The weather of the night: clear and cold, a light northerly; the moon (21 days) not up until about one.
